    <DataNode elementId="AT1G08980-MONOMER" textLabel="indole-3-acetamide amidohydrolase" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q9FR37" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="900" centerY="675" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">Indole-3-acetic acid (IAA) is a major form of the phytohormone auxin that is found throughout the plant kingdom |CITS: [15749753]|. This enzyme can catalyze the formation of IAA from indole-3-acetamide (IAM), a metabolite shown to exist in Arabidopsis thaliana grown under sterile conditions, demonstrating that this compound is not coming from bacterial contamination |CITS: [12430025]|. Unlike similar proteins found in other organisms which act as homodimers, AMI1 appears to exist as a monomer |CITS: [12620340][17555521]|. Ser137 is required for AMI1 enzymatic activity |CITS: [17555521]|. Purified recombinant AMI1 has its highest activity with indole-3-acetamide followed by L-asparagine, 1-naphthalene-acetamide, and nicotinamide |CITS:[12620340]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT1G08980-MONOMER" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT1G08980.1" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Panther" value="PTHR11895" />
